  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Wednesday) Issued at 843 PM CDT Wed Sep \n23 2026\n\nThrough the long term forecast, mid level ridging to our \nsouthwest will dampen, keeping troughing to our east as the \nprimary influence for local weather. The dampening ridge paired \nwith persistent troughing along the east coast will maintain \nnorthwest flow aloft through the period. This will maintain our \ncooler and drier airmass with temperatures remaining in the mid to\nhigh 80s each day. The next feature of interest looks to arrive \nat the end of the long term in the form of a passing mid level \ntrough and associated surface cold front. Along and ahead of this \nfront, light southerly surface flow looks to induce some weak \nreturn flow which may allow us to briefly break back into the 90s \non Tuesday, this however is contingent on the actual timing of the\nfront. If the front speeds up, associated cloud cover and slight \nincreases in rain chances may keep highs on Tuesday below 90. \nCurrently kept blended guidance for rain chances, which support \nthe front passing through on Wednesday with highest rain chances \nWednesday afternoon. With this being about a week out, timing and \nimpacts from the front are likely to change, please check back for\nupdates.",
